= Modal logic =

== Overview ==
'''Modal logic''' extends logical languages with operators that express modes such as necessity and possibility.

Related modal systems formalize knowledge, belief, time, obligation, action, and other intensional notions.

== Basic operators ==
In alethic modal logic:

<pre>
□P  — necessarily P
◇P  — possibly P
</pre>

These operators are not probability values.

“Necessarily” does not mean “very likely”.

== Possible-world semantics ==
Kripke semantics evaluates modal formulas across possible worlds or states connected by an accessibility relation.

A typical interpretation is:

<pre>
□P is true at world w
iff P is true at every world accessible from w
</pre>

and:

<pre>
◇P is true at w
iff P is true at some accessible world
</pre>

== Modal systems ==
Different constraints on accessibility produce different modal logics.

Well-known normal systems include K, T, S4, and S5.

No single modal system is the definition of modal logic as a field.

== Other modalities ==
Modal techniques support:

* epistemic logic — knowledge;
* doxastic logic — belief;
* temporal logic — time;
* deontic logic — obligation/permission;
* dynamic logic — action/program change.

The same formal notation can have different interpretations.

== Modal logic and ordinary language ==
Natural-language modal expressions involve context, scope, modality type, and pragmatic effects.

Formal modal systems idealize these phenomena rather than reproducing every feature of ordinary modal language.

